        "titulo": "The English speaker's trap",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: good news first: German possessives work like English ones — no article before them. \"My father\" is mein Vater, never der mein Vater. STEP 2: the owner picks the stem (my → mein, his → sein, her → ihr); the thing owned picks the ending (mein Vater, meine Mutter).",
        "comparacao": "English already gets the owner right: \"his father\", \"her father\". German does the same with sein and ihr. The difference: the German possessive also takes an ending that agrees with the thing owned — something English never does. So \"his mother\" is seine Mutter and \"her father\" is ihr Vater. And watch the triple meaning of ihr: «ihr» = her / their, «Ihr» (capital) = your (formal), and «ihr» = you all.",
