        "titulo": "Haben and the direct object",
        "explicacao": "«Haben» (to have) is irregular. The direct object of «haben» goes into the accusative. In the masculine singular, «ein» becomes «einen» and «kein» becomes «keinen»; in the feminine, neuter and plural nothing changes: «Ich habe einen Bruder. Ich habe eine Schwester. Ich habe ein Kind. Ich habe keine Kinder».",
        "comparacao": "English shows the same idea only in pronouns: \"he\" sees, but you see \"him\". German does it with articles too, but only for masculine nouns: «ein Bruder» (the one who is) becomes «einen Bruder» (the one you have) — like he → him.",
